10:22:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A telescope does “zoom in” in the sense that it makes an object look bigger, but zoom alone cannot create missing detail. Think of taking a photo with your phone: - If the original image contains clear pixels of a distant object, zooming/cropping can enlarge them. - But if that object was smaller than a pixel or blurred into the background, enlarging the image only makes a bigger blur—not a sharper object. Telescopes have the same issue. Light from a tiny distant object spreads out due to two main limits: 1. **Diffraction:** Light waves passing through a telescope’s opening cannot be focused into an infinitely small point. A larger mirror reduces this blur, but never eliminates it. 2. **Atmospheric turbulence:** For ground telescopes, moving air further smears the image. Adaptive optics helps, but does not make resolution unlimited. For the Moon flag, its apparent width is far smaller than the smallest detail even Hubble can distinguish. Hubble can make the Moon’s image large, but the flag’s light gets mixed into roughly the same image spot as the surrounding lunar soil. It cannot tell “this light came from the flag” versus “this light came from a patch of ground a few meters away.” To resolve a 1-meter object on the Moon in visible light, you would need an optical telescope with an aperture on the order of **100–200 meters across** in ideal conditions—far larger than ordinary telescopes and still extraordinarily challenging from Earth.
