10:23:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It sounds counterintuitive because “far away” and “small detail” are different problems. A distant galaxy can be billions of light-years away but still be **tens of thousands of light-years across**. Its apparent size in the sky can be much larger than a 1-meter flag on the Moon. Telescopes can detect and photograph its overall shape, bright stars, gas, or central black hole activity—not necessarily tiny details like individual planets, cars, or flags within it. For example: - A large galaxy might span **several arcseconds or even arcminutes** in the sky. - A Moon flag spans about **0.0008 arcseconds**. - That makes the flag thousands to millions of times harder to resolve than the visible overall structure of many distant galaxies. Also, galaxies contain enormous numbers of stars, so they emit a huge total amount of light. A flag is small, dim, and sits against bright lunar ground. A useful analogy: from far away, you might clearly see a brightly lit city skyline across a valley, but still be unable to read a tiny sign on a building only a few blocks away. The skyline is much bigger and brighter; the sign requires finer resolution. So telescopes are excellent at collecting faint light from huge distant objects, but resolving a tiny object requires a very large aperture or getting much closer.
