10:23:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No. “Super powerful” does not mean infinitely powerful. A telescope has a finite ability to distinguish two nearby directions in the sky. Its fundamental resolution is approximately: \[ \text{resolution} \approx \frac{\lambda}{D} \] where: - \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of light, - \(D\) is the telescope’s effective aperture diameter. A bigger aperture gives finer resolution, but only by a finite amount. And as an object gets farther away, its angular size gets smaller in direct proportion to distance. So, for a fixed 1-meter object: - Double the distance → it appears half as wide. - To resolve it at that doubled distance, you need roughly twice the aperture (at the same wavelength). - For arbitrarily large distances, you would need an arbitrarily large telescope. There is no finite telescope that can resolve *anything at any distance*. For the Moon flag, an ideal visible-light telescope would need an aperture on the order of a few hundred meters just to separate a roughly 1-meter feature from nearby ground. To clearly identify it as a flag, rather than a bright or dark patch, would require still better resolution and contrast. Existing telescopes are not that capable in visible light, especially through Earth’s atmosphere. Meanwhile, a galaxy billions of light-years away may be tens of thousands of light-years wide. Its *overall angular size* can be much larger than a one-meter flag’s. A telescope can therefore image the galaxy’s broad structure without being able to resolve tiny objects inside it. So “powerful” means a telescope has an impressive—but still specified and finite—combination of: - light-gathering ability, - angular resolution, - wavelength coverage, - and sensitivity. It does not mean unlimited zoom or unlimited detail.
