10:23:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That classroom statement was probably a simplified way of saying telescopes let us see very distant things—not a literal law of physics. Taken literally, “a strong enough telescope can see anything in perfect detail” is wrong. No telescope has unlimited resolution, and several limits apply: - **Diffraction:** finite aperture blurs light waves. - **Distance:** small objects occupy a smaller angle as they get farther away. - **Atmosphere:** blurs ground-based observations. - **Brightness and contrast:** an object may be too faint or blend into its background. - **Wavelength:** some details are only visible in certain kinds of light. - **Time and motion:** objects can move or change during an exposure. - **Physics:** at extreme distances, intervening dust, cosmic expansion, and finite light travel time matter. The accurate version is: > With a sufficiently large instrument, at an appropriate wavelength, and enough light, we can observe increasingly fine detail—but not arbitrary detail at arbitrary distance. This does not make telescope observations suspect. It means each observation has a well-defined capability. Astronomers can calculate what size feature an instrument should resolve, test it on known targets, calibrate it, and independently compare results. For the Moon specifically, we do not need Earth telescopes to investigate the Apollo sites. Lunar orbiters have photographed them from tens of kilometers above the surface, resolving landers, rover tracks, disturbed soil, and other hardware. The flags themselves are tiny, and their fabric may now be degraded, but their nonvisibility from Earth is exactly what ordinary optics predicts.
